        <li><strong>{s.name}.</strong> {s.text}</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>When scheduled pickup makes more sense than route-based pickup</h2>
    <p>Routine 'whenever a route is passing' pickup is fine for single small items — it just means you don't know exactly which day it'll be, only that it'll be soon.</p>
    <p>Scheduled pickup fits better when:</p>
    <p>You need pickup outside business hours (office pickups outside working time).</p>
    <p>Multiple people need to be available (apartment building coordinated pickup, office team member with drive-destruction access).</p>
    <p>Access is restricted to specific times (some buildings limit commercial vehicles to certain hours).</p>
    <p>There's a deadline (lease expiry, tax year-end, audit date, event date).</p>
    <p>The batch is large enough that route flexibility doesn't compensate for the coordination cost.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What affects slot availability</h2>
    <p>Your area — some Kochi neighbourhoods have more frequent scheduled routes than others.</p>
    <p>Job size — a 5-device pickup slots more easily than a 50-device pickup.</p>
    <p>Notice — 3-day-out requests have more slot options than same-day.</p>
    <p>Time preference — mid-day weekday slots are usually easier than early morning or weekends.</p>
    <p>Additional services — if the pickup includes on-site data destruction or requires specific transport (bulky items, refrigerant handling), fewer slots fit.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Recurring scheduled pickup for offices</h2>
    <p>Some offices set up recurring scheduled pickup — a monthly slot on a fixed day, so retired IT accumulates in a designated spot and gets picked up on a predictable schedule. Reduces admin overhead compared to booking each time.</p>
    <p>Common for offices with steady turnover of laptops (support desks, sales-team fleet management, contractor onboarding/offboarding). Contact if you'd like to set up recurring scheduling.</p>
  </section>

